Tony Crawford <tc13@kuerbis.dnsalias.net> wrote:
>Darwin seems to insist on having them in UTF-8. I can't seem to make it  
>display them in ISO-8859-15 encoding.

Does Darwin have a port of luit?

DESCRIPTION
       Luit  is a filter that can be run between an arbitrary applica-
       tion and a UTF-8 terminal emulator.  It will  convert  applica-
       tion  output from the locale's encoding into UTF-8, and convert
       terminal input from UTF-8 into the locale's encoding.

>Fortunately, GCC 4 and the iconv library are there, so the conversion  
>itself is a trivial problem.
>
>The question is, should I implement TTY output in UTF-8 encoding as a  
>compile-time option, or as a new windowing port based on the regular TTY  
>port, so that it's selectable at run-time?

The encoding should absolutely be detected from the locale at run-time;
many systems have users using a variety of encodings and there's really
no good reason for any program to have the encoding hardwired in at
compile time. Without knowing much about the relevant parts of Nethack,
but having a reasonable amount of experience with writing encoding-aware
code, I think encoding detection should be done across the board if the
necessary library support is available, perhaps with an option to
disable it. I'm not sure I see why this would be a new port rather than
just fixing the existing one.

  http://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~mgk25/unicode.html#activate

>If it's a new port, then the iconv library gets linked into the program  
>for on-the-fly conversion, creating either a dynamically-linked library  
>dependency (not likely to be a problem on systems modern enough to even  
>have a UTF-8 locale)

Indeed many such systems will have an iconv-a-like in their libc, so it
won't make any significant difference there.

